      "label": "supported"
    },
    {
      "item_id": "A.c2",
      "evidence_quote": "the risk of death (risk ratio [95% CI]) was 1.12 (0.94-1.34) in the CARD-only group... significant survival differences were again found between GM-only and the MIXED group (1.25 [1.14-1.37]).",
      "reason": "The passage provides the exact risk ratios and confidence intervals for the CARD-only group and the second cohort's GM-only versus MIXED group comparison, supporting the statement.",
      "label": "supported"
    },
    {
      "item_id": "A.l1",
      "evidence_quote": "In a retrospective cohort study conducted with national Veterans Health Administration (VHA) databases, we examined the association between the pattern of outpatient care and survival",
      "reason": "The passage confirms the study is a retrospective cohort of veterans examining an association. Describing the inherent limitations of this study design (residual confounding, limited generalizability, and lack of causation) is allowed.",
      "label": "supported"
    },
    {
      "item_id": "A.l2",
      "evidence_quote": "Adjusting for differences in patient and hospital characteristics markedly reduced the ORs... Using IV methods... we found an even smaller, statistically insignificant association between physician specialty and one-year mortality",
      "reason": "The passages confirm the studies are about acute myocardial infarction and that mortality differences by specialty shrank or became non-significant after adjusting for patient characteristics.",
      "label": "supported"
    },
    {
      "item_id": "B.c1",
      "evidence_quote": "lowest for patients in the MIXED group (19%). By use of the MIXED group as reference... the risk of death (risk ratio [95% CI]) was 1.12 (0.94-1.34) in the CARD-only group, 1.26 (1.15-1.38) in the GM-only group, and 1.48 (1.28-1.72) in the no-GM/CARD group.",
      "reason": "The passage directly supports all the claims and numbers regarding the survival rates and adjusted risk ratios for the different care groups.",
      "label": "supported"
    },
    {
      "item_id": "B.c2",
      "evidence_quote": "Cohort-2 results were consistent with cohort 1 for most covariates, and significant survival differences were again found between GM-only and the MIXED group (1.25 [1.14-1.37]).",
      "reason": "The passage explicitly reports the replication of the survival difference in the second cohort with the exact risk ratio and confidence interval provided.",
      "label": "supported"
    },
    {
      "item_id": "B.c3",
      "evidence_quote": "These patients had a 10 percent... lower absolute mortality rate at one year. After multivariate adjustment... adjusted mortality difference was 2 percent... Using IV methods... statistically insignificant association",
      "reason": "The passage confirms that the initial lower mortality rate for cardiologist care largely disappeared after adjustment and became statistically insignificant when using instrumental variables.",
      "label": "supported"
    },
    {
      "item_id": "B.l1",
      "evidence_quote": "In a retrospective cohort study conducted with national Veterans Health Administration (VHA) databases, we examined the association",
      "reason": "The passage states the study is a retrospective cohort of VA patients examining an association. Noting the standard limitations of this study design (residual confounding and lack of established causality) is supported.",
      "label": "supported"
    },
    {
      "item_id": "B.l2",
      "evidence_quote": "the risk of death (risk ratio [95% CI]) was 1.12 (0.94-1.34) in the CARD-only group",
      "reason": "The passage shows that the difference in mortality between cardiology-only and mixed care was not statistically significant. This contradicts the statement's conclusion that the benefit is tied to combined care rather than specialty care alone, as specialty care alone was not shown to be significantly worse.",
      "label": "unsupported"
    },
    {
      "item_id": "B.l3",
      "evidence_quote": "Patients admitted by cardiologists had fewer comorbid conditions and less severe AMIs.",
      "reason": "The passages confirm the studies focus on acute myocardial infarction and that patients treated by cardiologists had fewer comorbidities, supporting the idea that patient differences may explain the apparent advantages.",
      "label": "supported"
    }
  ]
}

